  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1214 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nAs of noon, satellite shows stratus continuing to break up over\ncentral Nebraska and Kansas. After a cool start to the day,\ntemperatures should be able to rebound to the mid 40s to mid 50s\nthis afternoon. \n\nStratus and fog is then expected to return tonight. Most near-\nterm models depict at least some visibility reduction across the\nmajority of our area through mid-morning on Saturday. If these\ntrends continue, another Dense Fog Advisory may be needed.\nForecast low temperatures have trended a bit warmer thanks to\nthe fog and cloud potential tonight. Even areas that remain\nfoggy and manage to dip to 32 shouldn't have any issues slick\nroads given that soil temperatures are still in the 45-55 degree\nrange. \n\nBeyond tonight, the threat for freezing temperatures is minimal\nthrough next week as we remain in a relatively warm pattern. \nGlobal ensembles favor the 7-Day AVERAGE temperature anomalies \nto be in the +7-10 degree range...which is pretty substantial. \nNormal high temperatures for early November is the 50s, and\nshould see lots of days with highs in the 60s. Additionally,\nwinds look to remain relatively light through most of next week,\nmaking for a very pleasant November week. \n\nPrecipitation chances are also minimal through next week. The\nEPS and GEFS are both \"bone dry\" through the middle of next\nweek, with only a handful of members showing light rain next\nThursday (5% chance). Even beyond that, rainfall looks pretty \nlimited through the following week before potentially increasing\nin the 19th-21st timeframe.",
